# Pocket ID 2.13.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins ghcr.io/pocket-id/pocket-id:v2.13.0 (image built
# 2026-08-07; multi-arch index digest
# sha256:c9c1d7b70006968d673a1073df048a3fdec73ccb54e182320b56bb911340f6f1,
# which resolves on ghcr.io today).
#
# Source: the `EnvConfigSchema` struct tags in
# backend/internal/common/env_config.go at v2.13.0, plus the four bare
# `os.Getenv` sites outside it (backend/internal/tracing/telemetry_controller.go,
# backend/internal/bootstrap/observability_boostrap.go,
# backend/internal/appconfig/service.go, and the `_FILE` resolver in
# env_config.go), plus scripts/docker/entrypoint.sh for PUID/PGID.
# Cross-checked against pocket-id.org/docs/configuration/environment-variables.
#
# THIS IS AN OIDC IDENTITY PROVIDER. Whoever controls this instance can mint
# tokens for, and therefore sign in as, any user in every application behind
# it. Three things follow:
#
#   1. FIRST-RUN BOOTSTRAP IS AN OPEN PAGE, NOT A CODE. Immediately after the
#      first boot, `<APP_URL>/setup` is reachable by anyone and creates the
#      first administrator with no authentication (frontend route
#      frontend/src/routes/setup; API `POST /api/signup/setup`; guarded only
#      by "no user exists yet": usersignup/service.go locks the users table
#      and returns setup_already_completed once one does). There is no
#      one-time code printed in the logs at v2; that was the v1.x flow. Claim
#      the admin account the moment the container is up, before the URL is
#      public. (`pocket-id one-time-access-token` on an existing instance
#      prints an `<APP_URL>/lc/<token>` sign-in link; that is a recovery path,
#      not the bootstrap.)
#   2. APP_URL IS SECURITY-RELEVANT, NOT COSMETIC. It is the OIDC issuer
#      (`Issuer`/`BaseURL`/`AccessTokenIssuer` in
#      bootstrap/services_bootstrap.go), the base of every URL in
#      /.well-known/openid-configuration, and the base of email login links.
#      It also decides cookie hardening: utils/cookie/cookie_names.go drops
#      the `__Host-`/`__Secure-` prefixes from the session, access-token,
#      device and reauthentication cookies whenever APP_URL is a plain-HTTP
#      origin. Set it to the exact public HTTPS origin, with no path (that is
#      validated), or tokens verify against the wrong issuer and the cookies
#      lose their prefix protection.
#   3. ENCRYPTION_KEY IS MANDATORY SINCE v2.0. Under 16 bytes and the process
#      logs "Configuration error" and exits 1 (ValidateEnvConfig). It encrypts
#      the token-signing private keys now held in the database. Losing it
#      loses every signing key; rotating it needs the
#      `pocket-id encryption-key-rotate --new-key` CLI, not just a restart.
#
# Keys below that RELAX trust, called out where they are declared:
# TRUST_PROXY, PROXY_PROTOCOL, TRUSTED_PLATFORM (believe forwarded client-IP
# headers), ALLOW_INSECURE_CALLBACK_URLS, DISABLE_RATE_LIMITING,
# STATIC_API_KEY, ALLOW_DOWNGRADE, SMTP_SKIP_CERT_VERIFY,
# LDAP_SKIP_CERT_VERIFY, EMAIL_ONE_TIME_ACCESS_AS_UNAUTHENTICATED_ENABLED,
# WEBAUTHN_USER_VERIFICATION, WEBAUTHN_ALLOW_SYNCED_PASSKEYS, and the
# ANALYTICS_DISABLED / VERSION_CHECK_DISABLED phone-home pair.
#
# Where the upstream reference and the code disagree at this version:
#   - `SIGNUP_DEFAULT_USER_GROUP_IDS` in the docs is NOT the name the code
#     reads. The override name is derived from the JSON key
#     `signupDefaultUserGroupIDs` by utils.CamelCaseToScreamingSnakeCase,
#     whose acronym rule splits "IDs", so the variable actually looked up is
#     `SIGNUP_DEFAULT_USER_GROUP_I_DS`. Declared under the real name.
#   - `LDAP_SOFT_DELETE_USERS` is documented as defaulting to `false`; the
#     code default (appconfig/model.go getDefaultConfig) is `true`.
#   - The docs omit `APP_ENV`, `ALLOW_DOWNGRADE`, `SYSTEMD_SOCKET`,
#     `ACTORS_HOST`, `ACTORS_PORT`, and the `_FILE` twins of
#     `DB_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STATIC_API_KEY` and `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`,
#     all of which the code reads.
#   - `NOTIFY_SOCKET` is read (utils/systemd/sdnotify.go) but is set by the
#     systemd supervisor, never by an operator, and is inert in Docker, so it
#     is not declared here.
#
# The compose sets APP_URL, ENCRYPTION_KEY and TRUST_PROXY. Everything else is
# optional at this version; an instance with just those three boots on SQLite
# under /app/data.
#
# UPGRADE NOTE: moving an existing v1.x deployment onto this image is a
# migration, not a bump: ENCRYPTION_KEY becomes mandatory, JWKs move from
# disk into the database (users may have to re-authenticate), KEYS_STORAGE /
# KEYS_PATH / DB_PROVIDER are gone, LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_ADMIN_GROUP is now
# LDAP_ADMIN_GROUP_NAME, and callback-URL wildcards are matched more strictly.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Environment Variables", https://pocket-id.org/docs/configuration/environment-variables)
# ---

# ─── Core ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The public origin Pocket ID is reached at, and the OIDC issuer every client
# validates tokens against. Must have no path; a trailing slash is trimmed and
# the value is lowercased. A plain-HTTP value silently downgrades the session,
# access-token, device and reauthentication cookies by dropping their
# `__Host-`/`__Secure-` prefixes, so use HTTPS in production.
# @required @type=url
# @docs("Installation", https://pocket-id.org/docs/setup/installation)
APP_URL=

# At least 16 bytes; the app exits 1 at boot without it. Encrypts the
# token-signing private keys stored in the database. `openssl rand -base64 32`
# is the documented generator. Keep it with your backups: lose it and the
# instance cannot decrypt its own signing keys. Change it only through
# `pocket-id encryption-key-rotate --new-key <new>`, then update this value.
# @required @sensitive @type=string(minLength=16)
# @docs("Migrate to v2.0", https://pocket-id.org/docs/setup/major-releases/migrate-v2)
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Read the encryption key from this path instead of the variable (Docker
# secrets). The file is read as raw bytes, so a trailing newline becomes part
# of the key and will not match what `encryption-key-rotate` was given.
ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=

# Baked into the image as `production`. `development` or `test` turn off the
# analytics heartbeat and change internal behavior; never set on a real
# deployment.
# @type=enum(production, development, test)
APP_ENV=production

# Overrides only the URLs published in /.well-known/openid-configuration, for
# the case where OIDC clients reach Pocket ID on a different address than end
# users do. Defaults to APP_URL. Must have no path.
# @type=url
INTERNAL_APP_URL=

# ─── Reverse proxy and client IP ──────────────────────────────────────────

# Which proxies may be believed when they claim a client IP via
# `X-Forwarded-For` / `X-Real-IP`. Client IP feeds rate limiting and the audit
# log, so trusting the wrong source lets a caller forge both. `true` (the
# compose value) trusts 0.0.0.0/0 and ::/0 and is only safe when Pocket ID is
# unreachable except through your proxy; prefer a comma-separated list of the
# proxy IPs or CIDRs that actually connect to it.
TRUST_PROXY=true

# Proxies allowed to send PROXY-protocol headers ahead of the HTTP
# connection. Same trust question as TRUST_PROXY, same `true`/`false`/CIDR
# list syntax. Requires a TCP listener: mutually exclusive with UNIX_SOCKET.
PROXY_PROTOCOL=

# Takes the client IP straight from one named header and skips
# `X-Forwarded-For` parsing entirely: `CF-Connecting-IP` for Cloudflare,
# `Fly-Client-IP` for Fly.io, `X-Appengine-Remote-Addr` for App Engine, or a
# custom name. Anything that can reach the port can then set that header, so
# only use it when the platform strips it at the edge.
TRUSTED_PLATFORM=

# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SQLite file path or a `postgres://` / `postgresql://` DSN; the provider is
# inferred from the prefix (DB_PROVIDER was removed in v2.0). Empty means
# SQLite at `data/pocket-id.db`, which is inside the container's /app/data
# volume. Do not put the SQLite file on NFS/SMB.
# @sensitive
DB_CONNECTION_STRING=

# Read the connection string from this path instead (Docker secrets).
DB_CONNECTION_STRING_FILE=

# Refuses to start if the database schema is newer than this binary, so a
# rollback cannot silently run migrations backwards. Setting it true permits
# that downgrade; expect data loss.
# @type=boolean
ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=false

# ─── File storage (profile pictures, logos) ───────────────────────────────

# Where uploaded images live. `database` keeps them in the same store as
# everything else, which is what a single-volume deployment usually wants.
# @type=enum(filesystem, database, s3)
FILE_BACKEND=filesystem

# Directory for uploads when FILE_BACKEND is `filesystem` (also used as the
# local staging path for `s3`). Relative to /app, so the default sits inside
# the mounted data volume. Move it out and uploads stop surviving restarts.
UPLOAD_PATH=data/uploads

# S3 target, all four required when FILE_BACKEND=s3. S3_ENDPOINT is what
# points these at MinIO or R2 instead of AWS.
S3_BUCKET=
S3_REGION=
# @type=url
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# @sensitive
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Read the S3 secret from this path instead (Docker secrets).
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
# Path-style addressing (`endpoint/bucket/key`), which MinIO and most
# S3-compatible servers need.
# @type=boolean
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false
# Skips the checksum headers the AWS SDK adds by default; needed by some
# S3-compatible servers that reject them.
# @type=boolean
S3_DISABLE_DEFAULT_INTEGRITY_CHECKS=false

# ─── Listener and TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The compose exposes container port 1411 with no override, so this is that
# port's twin: change it here without changing the exposed port and routing
# breaks.
# @type=port
PORT=1411

# Bind address. `0.0.0.0` is what the container network needs; narrowing it
# makes the service unreachable from the proxy.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# Listen on a Unix socket instead of TCP; PORT and HOST are ignored when set.
# Not usable through the compose's published port.
UNIX_SOCKET=
# Permission bits for that socket, e.g. `660`.
UNIX_SOCKET_MODE=
# Take the listener from systemd socket activation. Mutually exclusive with
# UNIX_SOCKET, and meaningless under Docker.
# @type=boolean
SYSTEMD_SOCKET=

# Terminate TLS in the container itself rather than at a proxy. TLS_CERT and
# TLS_KEY carry PEM inline and are never reloaded; TLS_CERT_FILE and
# TLS_KEY_FILE are paths that must exist inside the container and are watched
# for changes. Each pair is all-or-nothing and the two pairs cannot be mixed.
TLS_CERT=
# @sensitive
TLS_KEY=
TLS_CERT_FILE=
TLS_KEY_FILE=

# Internal actor-system listener (background jobs: LDAP sync, SCIM sync,
# cleanup). Not published by the compose and not meant to be reachable from
# outside the container.
# @type=port
ACTORS_PORT=1414
ACTORS_HOST=0.0.0.0

# ─── Audit log and IP geolocation ─────────────────────────────────────────

# How long audit entries are kept. Must be greater than 0 or the app refuses
# to start. This is the record of who signed in to what, so shortening it
# shortens your incident window.
# @type=number
AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Free MaxMind GeoLite2 key used to resolve audit-log IPs to a location.
# Without it locations read "unknown"; nothing else breaks.
# @sensitive
# @docs("GeoLite2 sign up", https://www.maxmind.com/en/geolite2/signup)
MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY=

# Read the MaxMind key from this path instead (Docker secrets).
MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY_FILE=

# Where the downloaded GeoLite2 city database is cached, relative to /app.
# The default is inside the data volume, so it survives restarts.
GEOLITE_DB_PATH=data/GeoLite2-City.mmdb

# Mirror to fetch the GeoLite2 archive from. Empty uses MaxMind's own
# download endpoint with MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY substituted into it; only set
# this for an internal mirror.
# @type=url
GEOLITE_DB_URL=

# Extra IPv6 CIDRs to treat as "local" when labelling audit-log entries.
# Comma-separated; each must parse as an IPv6 range or the app exits.
LOCAL_IPV6_RANGES=

# ─── API access and rate limiting ─────────────────────────────────────────

# A fixed admin API key, materialised as a hidden "Static API User" account
# with full administrative rights over the identity provider. Anyone holding
# it can create users and OIDC clients. Must be at least 16 characters when
# set. Prefer per-user API keys issued in the UI; use this only for
# declarative provisioning.
# @sensitive @type=string(minLength=16)
# @docs("API", https://pocket-id.org/docs/api)
STATIC_API_KEY=

# Read the static API key from this path instead (Docker secrets).
STATIC_API_KEY_FILE=

# Turns off the built-in rate limiter that protects sign-in, one-time-access
# codes and the device flow from brute force. As of v2.13.0 the limiter also
# applies to loopback addresses. Only disable it if your proxy enforces an
# equivalent policy.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Hardening", https://pocket-id.org/docs/advanced/hardening)
DISABLE_RATE_LIMITING=false

# Whether OIDC clients may register plain-HTTP callback URLs on non-loopback
# hosts, where an authorization code can be intercepted in transit. Still
# defaults to true at v2 for compatibility (upstream plans false in v3); set
# it to false unless a specific client needs it. Loopback HTTP callbacks stay
# allowed either way.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Callback URL wildcards", https://pocket-id.org/docs/advanced/callback-url-wildcards)
ALLOW_INSECURE_CALLBACK_URLS=true

# ─── Phone-home ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A heartbeat is sent to Pocket ID's analytics endpoint every 24 hours to
# count running instances (only when APP_ENV is production). Set true to stop
# it. Required for air-gapped installs.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Analytics", https://pocket-id.org/docs/configuration/analytics)
ANALYTICS_DISABLED=false

# Periodic check against the GitHub releases API for a newer version. Set
# true for air-gapped installs or anywhere outbound traffic is audited.
# @type=boolean
VERSION_CHECK_DISABLED=false

# ─── Logging ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# @type=enum(debug, info, warn, error)
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Emit structured JSON log lines instead of human-readable text.
# @type=boolean
LOG_JSON=false

# Includes SQL query parameter VALUES in debug logs and traces. Those
# parameters carry tokens, password hashes and personal data. Troubleshooting
# only, and turn it back off afterwards.
# @type=boolean
LOG_QUERY_ARGS=false

# ─── Container user ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Read by scripts/docker/entrypoint.sh, not by the Go binary: the uid/gid the
# process drops to, and the owner it chowns /app/data to before starting.
# Only takes effect when the container starts as root.
# @type=number
# @docs("Understanding PUID and PGID", https://docs.linuxserver.io/general/understanding-puid-and-pgid/)
PUID=1000
# @type=number
PGID=1000

# ─── Observability (OpenTelemetry) ────────────────────────────────────────

# Read by the OpenTelemetry Go SDK bundled in the image, and by Pocket ID
# itself in bootstrap/observability_boostrap.go and
# tracing/telemetry_controller.go to decide what to enable. All export is off
# unless an exporter is named. Setting OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=otlp with a
# resolvable OTLP/HTTP endpoint also turns on browser-side tracing, proxied
# through the backend at /internal/telemetry/traces.
# @type=enum(otlp, none)
# @docs("OpenTelemetry SDK environment variables", https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/configuration/sdk-environment-variables/)
OTEL_TRACES_EXPORTER=
# `prometheus` starts a SECOND HTTP server exposing /metrics instead of
# pushing OTLP. That server is not published by the compose.
# @type=enum(otlp, prometheus, none)
OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=
# @type=enum(otlp, none)
OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER=
# Base collector endpoint used by whichever exporters are set to `otlp`.
# @type=url
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
# Overrides the base endpoint for traces only; it, or the base, is what
# enables frontend tracing.
# @type=url
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=
# Bind address and port of the Prometheus scrape server, when
# OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=prometheus. The default binds loopback inside the
# container, so a scraper outside it sees nothing until this is 0.0.0.0 and
# the port is published.
OTEL_EXPORTER_PROMETHEUS_HOST=localhost
# @type=port
OTEL_EXPORTER_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9464

# ─── UI-configuration overrides ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below is normally edited in the admin UI and stored in the
# database. The variables are read ONLY when UI_CONFIG_DISABLED=true
# (appconfig/service.go loadDbConfigFromEnv); with it false they are ignored
# entirely, and with it true the UI settings pages become read-only and any
# variable you leave unset falls back to its default rather than to whatever
# is in the database.

# @type=boolean
UI_CONFIG_DISABLED=false

# Product name shown in the UI and in emails.
APP_NAME=Pocket ID

# How long a signed-in session lasts, in minutes, before a passkey is needed
# again.
# @type=number
SESSION_DURATION=60

# Where users land after signing in. A path, or an absolute URL.
HOME_PAGE_URL=/settings/account

# Marks new or changed email addresses as verified without sending a
# verification mail. Only safe when the addresses come from a source you
# already trust, such as LDAP.
# @type=boolean
EMAILS_VERIFIED=false

# Any CSS color (hex, rgb, hsl) for the UI accent, or `default`.
ACCENT_COLOR=default

# @type=boolean
DISABLE_ANIMATIONS=false

# Whether users may change their own name, email and username. Turning it off
# is the usual choice when LDAP is the source of truth.
# @type=boolean
ALLOW_OWN_ACCOUNT_EDIT=true

# Self-registration. `open` lets anyone who can reach the sign-up page create
# an account in your identity provider; `withToken` requires an admin-issued
# signup token. Leave `disabled` unless you mean it.
# @type=enum(disabled, withToken, open)
# @docs("User management", https://pocket-id.org/docs/setup/user-management)
ALLOW_USER_SIGNUPS=disabled

# Groups granted automatically to users who self-register, as a JSON array of
# group UUIDs, e.g. `["a3888f2b-4c00-4b23-9c85-a3c8d685eb1f"]`. NOTE the
# name: the docs print `SIGNUP_DEFAULT_USER_GROUP_IDS`, but the code derives
# `SIGNUP_DEFAULT_USER_GROUP_I_DS` from the `signupDefaultUserGroupIDs` config
# key, and that is the only name it looks up. Since v2 restricts OIDC clients
# by group, this decides what a self-registered user can reach.
# @docs("Allowed user groups", https://pocket-id.org/docs/configuration/allowed-groups)
SIGNUP_DEFAULT_USER_GROUP_I_DS=[]

# Custom claims added to every self-registered user, as a JSON array of
# `{"key":…,"value":…}` objects. These claims are handed to relying parties,
# so anything an app authorizes on can be set here.
SIGNUP_DEFAULT_CUSTOM_CLAIMS=[]

# ─── Email (SMTP) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Whether an account must have an email address at all. Users without one
# cannot use any email-based flow.
# @type=boolean
REQUIRE_USER_EMAIL=true

SMTP_HOST=
# @type=port
SMTP_PORT=
# Envelope sender, e.g. `pocket-id@example.com`.
# @type=email
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_USER=
# @sensitive
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Read the SMTP password from this path instead (Docker secrets).
SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE=
# `tls` is implicit TLS (port 465), `starttls` upgrades a plaintext
# connection (587), `none` sends credentials in the clear.
# @type=enum(none, starttls, tls)
SMTP_TLS=none
# Accepts any SMTP server certificate, including one an attacker on the path
# presents. Self-signed internal relays only.
# @type=boolean
SMTP_SKIP_CERT_VERIFY=false

# Email the user when a sign-in happens from a new device.
# @type=boolean
EMAIL_LOGIN_NOTIFICATION_ENABLED=false

# Lets an admin email a login code to a user, bypassing that user's passkey.
# @type=boolean
EMAIL_ONE_TIME_ACCESS_AS_ADMIN_ENABLED=false

# Lets ANY user request an emailed login code instead of using a passkey.
# This makes the mailbox equivalent to the passkey: whoever reads the user's
# mail can sign in as them, everywhere Pocket ID is the IdP. The code is
# bound to the requesting browser (since v2.0) but that is the only extra
# barrier.
# @type=boolean
EMAIL_ONE_TIME_ACCESS_AS_UNAUTHENTICATED_ENABLED=false

# Warn users by email before an API key of theirs expires.
# @type=boolean
EMAIL_API_KEY_EXPIRATION_ENABLED=false

# Send a verification mail on signup and on every email change. Recommended
# whenever signups are open, since a verified address is what several flows
# key on.
# @type=boolean
EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ENABLED=false

# ─── LDAP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# @type=boolean
# @docs("LDAP", https://pocket-id.org/docs/configuration/ldap)
LDAP_ENABLED=false

# e.g. `ldaps://dc.example.com:636`. Plain `ldap://` sends the bind password
# and every synced attribute in the clear.
LDAP_URL=
LDAP_BIND_DN=
# @sensitive
LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=
# Read the bind password from this path instead (Docker secrets).
LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD_FILE=
# Search base DN for users and groups.
LDAP_BASE=
LDAP_USER_SEARCH_FILTER=(objectClass=person)
LDAP_USER_GROUP_SEARCH_FILTER=(objectClass=groupOfNames)
# Accepts any directory server certificate. On `ldaps://` this removes the
# only protection on the bind password.
# @type=boolean
LDAP_SKIP_CERT_VERIFY=false

# Attribute mapping. LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_UNIQUE_IDENTIFIER and
# LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_GROUP_UNIQUE_IDENTIFIER must name attributes that never
# change (objectGUID, entryUUID): if the value changes, the sync sees a new
# principal and the old account is orphaned or removed.
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_UNIQUE_IDENTIFIER=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_USERNAME=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_EMAIL=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_FIRST_NAME=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_LAST_NAME=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_DISPLAY_NAME=cn
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_USER_PROFILE_PICTURE=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_GROUP_MEMBER=member
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_GROUP_UNIQUE_IDENTIFIER=
LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_GROUP_NAME=

# Members of this directory group become Pocket ID administrators on every
# sync. Whoever can add a member to that group can take over the identity
# provider. Renamed from `LDAP_ATTRIBUTE_ADMIN_GROUP` in v2.0.
LDAP_ADMIN_GROUP_NAME=

# Users that disappear from LDAP are disabled rather than deleted. The code
# defaults this to `true` (the docs say `false`); `false` means a directory
# sync can delete Pocket ID accounts outright.
# @type=boolean
LDAP_SOFT_DELETE_USERS=true

# ─── WebAuthn (passkeys) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `required` makes the authenticator verify the human (biometric or PIN), so
# a stolen security key alone is not enough. `preferred` accepts a ceremony
# with no user verification, turning the passkey into a single factor. As of
# v2.12.0 this policy is actually enforced during ceremony validation
# (GHSA-pgqv-hh75-xx4x); before that it was advisory.
# @type=enum(required, preferred)
WEBAUTHN_USER_VERIFICATION=required

# Whether passkeys that sync through iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager
# or a password manager may be registered. Set false to require
# device-bound credentials.
# @type=boolean
WEBAUTHN_ALLOW_SYNCED_PASSKEYS=true

# `platform` restricts registration to the device's built-in authenticator,
# `cross-platform` to roaming keys such as a YubiKey, `any` allows both.
# @type=enum(any, platform, cross-platform)
WEBAUTHN_AUTHENTICATOR_ATTACHMENT=any

# ─── OIDC clients ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# JSON array of URL patterns from which an OAuth Client ID Metadata Document
# may be fetched, letting a client register itself by URL instead of being
# created by an admin. An empty array disables that entirely. Widen it only
# to hosts you control, since a matching URL becomes a client of your IdP.
CIMD_URL_ALLOWLIST=[]
